<text layer="text" matrix="1 0 0 1 10.5364 -48.0461" transformations="translations" pos="51.3982 772.29" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="482.568" height="18.866" depth="13.9" valign="top">For each new vertex, if we have more than one equivalence class with a triplet, it could lead to a cycle. 

What we can do, is to change the orientation of the new equivalence(s) so it goes with what we already havel. 

I will showcase the three possiblities for the case that we have two equivalence  classes.</text>
<text layer="case1changeText" transformations="translations" pos="61.0917 688.42" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="260.882" height="11.924" depth="6.95" valign="top">We can change the rotation of the new equivalence class, and it will fix the issue </text>
<use layer="case2Red" name="mark/disk(sx)" pos="161.239 458.684" size="large" stroke="UPred"/>
<use name="mark/disk(sx)" pos="238.958 353.844" size="normal" stroke="UPred"/>
<use name="mark/disk(sx)" pos="316.685 559.496" size="normal" stroke="UPred"/>
<use name="mark/disk(sx)" pos="394.405 458.681" size="large" stroke="UPred"/>
<text layer="case1changeText" matrix="1 0 0 1 -3.16092 0.00039273" transformations="translations" pos="64.2526 719.186" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="155.518" height="5.898" depth="0.91" valign="top">Case 1: </text>
<text layer="case2Changetext" matrix="1 0 0 1 0.0000093 -0.00000156" transformations="translations" pos="61.0917 688.42" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="260.882" height="11.924" depth="6.95" valign="top">We can change the rotation of the new equivalence class, and it will fix the issue </text>
<text matrix="1 0 0 1 -3.16092 -0.00000156" transformations="translations" pos="64.2526 719.186" stroke="black" type="minipage" width="155.518" height="5.898" depth="0.91" valign="top">Case 2: </text>
<text layer="conclusion1" matrix="1 0 0 1 0.0002833 0.00020332" transformations="translations" pos="49.5017 232.404" stroke="UPblue" type="minipage" width="495.633" height="60.933" depth="56.09" valign="top">Only one equivalent class can propagate beyond the smaller connected comp $H&apos;$ which means that also can propagate to other parts of the graph. 

So working on each triple doesn&apos;t affect any other triple from different connected comp triple, in the same adjacent edges, becasue only one eq will propagate and the choice of the (one/two) others won&apos;t change anything. So we can fix one by one. Which also mean we can prove my induction on the distinct vertices of the ajdacent edges, each vertex added add max 2i equivalent classes from all the triplet with the $\{0,...,I\}$ vertices and for each triplet we have only one eq, that propagate we can fix the odd crossings with the rest of the eq classes that don&apos;t propagate which means we can find a merge for all the new equivalence classes to keep the vertices have non-cyclic relation

The choice that we make for the triple doesn&apos;t affect other triple relations and it doesn&apos;t introduce any new odd crossings from the proof of the strong hanani-tutte</text>
